<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caonabo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1496">1496</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caonabo was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> of Maguana in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> and husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anacaona">Anacaona</a> of Jaragua. He is credited with destroying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>, the fort left by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> in 1493, and killing its garrison. Captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alonso-de-ojeda">Alonso de Ojeda</a> through a trick around 1495, he died at sea in 1496 while being sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Caonabo was not native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>: he was born among the Lucayan people of the Lucayan Archipelago of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> and later migrated to the island. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>, one of the first Spanish settlers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, wrote that because he stood out as a man of war and of peace he came to be king of that province and was highly esteemed by all. He ruled Maguana, one of the island's five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacicazgo">cacicazgos</a>, most likely from what is now San Juan de la Maguana in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1492 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s ship the Santa María was wrecked on the north coast on Christmas Day, and it was salvaged to build the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>, where Columbus left some of his crew. Caonabo's attack on the fort in 1493 left 39 Spaniards dead. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anacaona">Anacaona</a>, the sister of the cacique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohechio">Bohechío</a>, later explained that, angered at the Spaniards' treatment of the natives, she had moved him to it.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> returned and found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a> destroyed, Caonabo came to be considered one of the strongest leaders on the island; the cacique Guacanagaríx of Marién told the Spaniards he was responsible. Bartholomew Columbus then received word that Caonabo planned to attack the Spanish fort of Santo Tomás, and Columbus sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alonso-de-ojeda">Alonso de Ojeda</a> into the interior with four hundred men to subjugate the natives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Accounts of his capture differ, and the historian Samuel M. Wilson considers the story likely embellished and romanticised by the Spanish. In Las Casas's version Ojeda offered him polished handcuffs and chains as a gift, said to be worn by kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and locked them once Caonabo tried them on.</p><p>The capture led to the first Native American uprising against the Spanish. Caonabo's brother Manicatex gathered around 7,000 natives to rescue him but was defeated, largely through the Spaniards' use of cavalry, and taken prisoner. To prevent further risings the Spanish sent both brothers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and Caonabo died during the voyage and was buried at sea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caonabo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When he returned in late 1493 the fort had been destroyed and its men killed, an attack attributed to <b>Caonabo</b> of Maguana.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of her husband <b>Caonabo of Maguana</b>, Anacaona returned to Jaragua and, on Bohechío's death, became its ruler; she was celebrated for her poetry and areítos (songs and dances).</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-jaragua">Cacicazgo of Jaragua</a></li><li><blockquote>Its paramount cacique <b>Caonabo</b>, said by some sources to have come from the Lucayan islands, was known as a war leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-maguana">Cacicazgo of Maguana</a></li><li><blockquote>La Navidad was destroyed by <b>Caonabo of Maguana</b> in 1493.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-marien">Cacicazgo of Marién</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominican-republic">the Dominican Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19,-71&amp;year=1486" aria-label="Hispaniola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Caonabo"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4116 5892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4116 5892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19,-71&amp;year=1486">What was here in 1486? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
